The proof, since a widened timing budget is exactly the kind of change that
usually hides a defect rather than fixing one.

run_watchdog reaches the emergency drain through a loop bounded by the ultimate
deadline:

  wait_until(timeline.tauri_exit_deadline());
  final_action::run(...);                            // exits = 1
  wait_until(timeline.emergency_deadline());
  while Instant::now() < timeline.ultimate_deadline() {
      inventory.drain_once(actions.signaler.as_ref());   // signaler touched here
      ...
  }

Start the watchdog after that deadline and the loop body never runs once. The
signaler is never called at all — not late, never. exits still reaches 1, which
is why the first wait always succeeded and only the second one failed.

Both watchdog tests failed alternately on macOS CI for this one reason, and
that alternation was the tell: blocked_watchdog_cannot_delay_the_ultimate_exit
waits on signaler.entered, watchdog_requests_tauri_exit_then_starts_emergency_
drain waits on signaler.calls — the same event under two names.

Demonstrated by injecting a delay between the origin and the spawn, against the
new 400ms ultimate deadline:

  sleep 300ms  ->  ok
  sleep 500ms  ->  FAILED, "attente expirée : processus d'urgence signalé"

The flip lands on the deadline, on the predicted wait. Locally the test passed
40 times in isolation and 15 times on a fully saturated machine, so repetition
alone would never have found this — the setup simply has to outlast the whole
schedule, which a shared runner can do and this Mac does not.

Two things changed, both aimed at that cause. The origin is now taken as late
as possible, so no setup time is charged against the schedule the watchdog has
to walk. And the schedule is long enough to outlast a thread reaching the CPU.
Production is unaffected: it works in seconds (8/10/13/15), where scheduling
latency is noise.

wait_until's bound went to 10s for a different reason: it guards against a hung
test, it does not measure anything, so it should sit far above the schedule
rather than be tuned to it.

Whole Rust suite on macOS: 3668 passed, 0 failed, 3 ignored.
